WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.631
Your program wants to print ok and a newline.

2
00:00:05.631 --> 00:00:10.841
That's three bytes, written to file descriptor 1, the program's standard output.

3
00:00:11.067 --> 00:00:13.579
It can't drive the terminal itself.

4
00:00:13.579 --> 00:00:20.040
The CPU runs it in user mode, and user mode refuses the instructions that talk to devices.

5
00:00:20.267 --> 00:00:23.642
Only one program runs in kernel mode: the kernel.

6
00:00:23.642 --> 00:00:27.085
So your program has to ask it, with a system call.

7
00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:33.235
First the C library loads registers, the CPU's own small storage slots.

8
00:00:33.235 --> 00:00:36.328
The number for write, 64, goes in x8.

9
00:00:36.328 --> 00:00:39.672
The three arguments go in x0, x1 and x2.

10
00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:42.777
Then it runs one special instruction, svc.

11
00:00:42.777 --> 00:00:50.517
The CPU saves where the program was, switches to kernel mode, and jumps to an entry point the kernel set at boot.

12
00:00:50.517 --> 00:00:53.257
The program can't choose where it lands.

13
00:00:53.257 --> 00:00:54.284
This is a trap.

14
00:00:54.500 --> 00:01:01.227
The kernel reads x8, finds entry 64 in its system call table, and runs the write handler.

15
00:01:01.433 --> 00:01:08.012
The handler checks that file descriptor 1 is open, and that the buffer really is the program's own memory.

16
00:01:08.012 --> 00:01:10.556
It never trusts a pointer from user mode.

17
00:01:10.767 --> 00:01:14.293
Then it copies the three bytes and hands them to the terminal.

18
00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:19.033
It puts the result, 3 bytes written, in x0, and runs eret.

19
00:01:19.033 --> 00:01:24.113
The CPU drops back to user mode, right after the svc instruction.

20
00:01:24.333 --> 00:01:27.873
The C library returns 3, and the program carries on.

21
00:01:27.873 --> 00:01:29.915
It never touched the hardware.

22
00:01:30.133 --> 00:01:33.194
The crossing costs about 150 nanoseconds.

23
00:01:33.194 --> 00:01:34.762
Once, that's nothing.

24
00:01:34.762 --> 00:01:41.780
A logger that writes a million lines with one call each spends 150 milliseconds just crossing.

25
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.844
Every trip into the kernel goes through that one door, and every trip costs time.

26
00:01:46.844 --> 00:01:50.312
So batch small writes into big ones, and cross less often.
